Limit: the adopted Hermes CEO runtime has no signer for the Buzz ingress secret, no new signing platform, secret or external actor may be created, and a plain public pubkey is not authentication, so the CEO proves its Buzz channel identity by posting a one-time challenge token, minted for its admitted tool connection, in a Buzz mention its own Nostr key signs
Limit: pending challenges live only in daemon memory with no new table: a daemon restart drops them and the CEO asks again; one pending challenge per session, a new one replaces it, and each expires ten minutes after it was minted
Ruled-out: a Gateway identity field reporting the CEO's pubkey | it is a self-report, the Gateway describing itself, and not proof that the runtime holds the key
Ruled-out: an operator bind of the CEO's pubkey | a human attestation that would have to be repeated at every CEO generation
Warn: the key is write-once per session row and otherSessionCarrying refuses a key any other row holds, so after the next CEO re-adoption onto a new session this same key cannot be rebound without a further, separately approved change
Warn: a verified event addressed to the CTO whose content carries exactly one acp-buzz-bind token goes to the binding and never to admission, whoever signed it, so a message that quotes a live token is delivered to no one
Warn: a refused binding attempt writes one SESSION_BUZZ_ACTOR_BIND_REFUSED audit row per challenge and cause, and an unknown nonce writes none, so a stranger reposting a public token or a relay re-sending an event adds no audit row; the subscriber's admission-refused counter still counts every refused frame
Unverified: the challenge store's own verifyEvent re-check is not independently witnessed: removing it fails no test, because the mention subscriber's verifyEvent runs first on every event that reaches the store
Unverified: not run against a live relay, the Buzz CLI or a live Hermes Gateway; the tests state the process tree and the Gateway readback while the nostr-tools events, the mention subscriber, the adopted CEO tool socket and the registries are real
